@CORPFULL IS VICTIM OF HUGE SCAM--IT PAID OVER @AMOUNT MILLION FOR MINING RIGHTS TO A PHONY COPPER MINE IN THE RAINFORESTS OF IRIAN JAYA

@CORPFULL, a major mining and smelting company in the non-ferrous metals industry, has been hoodwinked out of @AMOUNT. it paid for the mining rights to a supposed "mountain of copper" at a remote rainforest location in Indonesia's Irian Jaya. The company, upon beginning mining operations in the area, after several pitched battles with local headhunters (which cost the lives of 20 miners), quickly discovered that the "mountain of copper" they had bought had been heavily "salted" with high-grade copper ore by a group of brilliant con men who claimed they had made the greatest copper discovery in the world.

The company took strong precautions against fraud, by sending in several experts to assay the property before purchasing the mining rights, but it turns out that the con men offered the experts a 20% cut in the @AMOUNT million they were asking for the sale of the mining rights, to buy their cooperation with the scam. Apparently, that was far too much money to resist, so @CORPFULL's experts went along with the con men, facilitating what is now being described as one of the biggest mining frauds in history, in an industry long known for such dubious practices.

In addition to paying the scamsters @AMOUNT. for the rights to the bogus copper mine, the company spent considerable additional sums to pay bribes to units of the Indonesian Army, which was finally brought in to protect the miners from local headhunters. The locals had fiercely resisted, as the company built a highway from the coast through their villages, to the mine development site on what the natives call the "Sacred Mountain of the Dark God." The military was only able to pacify the natives by use of airstrikes, heavy artillery bombardments and by sending in an armored division, which leveled all the native villages in the area and left few, if any survivors.

The company's stock price plunged on the news, as the losses incurred in writing off the worthless mining rights may prove to be financially crippling to @CORPFULL.

