Source (EN):
@CORPFULL SANCTIONED FOR UNSCRUPULOUS TACTICS, IN DENYING HEALTH INSURANCE CLAIMS OF PATIENTS FOR EMERGENCY ROOM TREATMENT

@CORPFULL, a health insurer with national operations in the U.S., has come under heavy criticism for its harsh policy of denying claims of patients who go to a hospital emergency room, if it turns out the patient did not have a life-threatening or serious condition that required immediate treatment. By doing so, and allowing policyholders whose claims are denied to be stuck with huge hospital bills, making an example of them, the company has found that it not only saves itself money from denying the claims, but terrorizes other policyholders, who then become afraid to go to a hospital emergency room if they are not absolutely certain that their condition is one that requires emergency treatment, and will be covered by their health insurance.

It has been a financial bonanza for the company, as the number of claims submitted to it for emergency care have decreased precipitously, but this has caused a number of deaths of individuals who had severe health conditions and should have gone in for emergency care, but were afraid to, and in a large number of cases where patients mis-diagnosed themselves and did go to an emergency room, they have been financially devastated or bankrupted when the deceitful insurance company refused to pay their claims.

As a result of investigations by the attorneys general of the 37 states where @CORP operates, the company has had its licenses to sell health insurance in those states revoked, due to its unethical and, in some cases, highly illegal, benefit denial practices.

In addition, major class action lawsuits have been filed on behalf of all of the company's health insurance policyholders, alleging "bad faith" in issuing insurance policies where the company intended to avoid paying the promised benefits, as was outlined in an emailed internal memorandum that was recently leaked to investigators by an anonymous whistleblower or hacker.

The reaction to this news on Wall Street was quite predictable, as the company's shares swooned, when investors realized its most profitable division was no longer going to generate anything but red ink.

