Source (EN):
@CORPFULL BUCKLES UNDER PRESSURE FROM THE WORLD COMMUNITY, ABANDONS HUGE IRIAN JAYA COPPER MINE

@CORPFULL, a major non-ferrous metals producer and smelter, finally caved in today to pressure from the U.N. Environmental Agency and other governmental and international organizations which had threatened to impose economic sanctions against the chaotic government of Indonesia, if the gigantic Irian Jaya open pit copper mine that was just recently opened by @CORPFULL were not closed down.

The mine, which has the largest copper ore body in the world, has been controversial from the start, as it is located in a pristine rainforest preserve near the coast, in an area that had been previously designated a U.N. World Heritage Site. In addition, the local indigenous tribe that lived at the foot of the mountain that was being mined was bitterly opposed to the strip mining of what had been a sacred mountain, in their tribal religion, and they fought two pitched battles with the miners in attempt to halt road construction and the opening of the mine itself. Reportedly, several of @CORPFULL's executives were eaten by some of the natives, who have a long tradition of ritual cannibalism, and are said to favor the "other white meat," referred to by the tribe as "long pig."

The resistance of the native tribe was overcome only after the company paid off the Indonesian government, which "re-located" the tribe by bringing in the Indonesian Air Force, which napalm-bombed the native villages, and appears to have wiped out the entire tribe, despite harsh condemnation by the world community. Apparently, the cannibal tribe was "re-located" into the hereafter by the Indonesian military.

@CORPFULL initially resisted pressure from all over the world, but finally had little choice but to close down its mine, when the threat of U.N. sanctions against the Indonesian government made it clear that the government would soon revoke the company's mining concession.  The company's losses on the abandonment of the Irian Jaya copper mine are expected to be as much as @AMOUNT.@MISC, which include the original purchase price for the mine plus major development costs incurred by @CORPFULL.

The company's costs also included amounts billed to it by the Indonesian Air Force for the military equipment lost and ordnance used in conflicts with the native tribe, in the process of "clearing" the mine site for the company.

